From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 23:31:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/33] wasm: drain the OPFS mirror before backup import
 returns
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The unpacked blobs of a backup import only reach the memfs; the OPFS
write-through that survives a reload is done by an async flusher. A reload
before that queue drains rebuilds the memfs from an incomplete OPFS, so a
freshly restored account is missing its pictures until they are re-fetched
(#77).

Awaiting `tokio::fs::flush_pending()` at the end of
`import_backup_stream_inner` closes that window in the one place both entry
points pass through — the file import and the second-device transfer receive
— and, because the caller emits ImexProgress(1000) only after this function
returns, it also makes the progress bar honest: 100% now means durable
instead of "unpacked, and some unknown amount of writing still to do" (#90).

Honest is not the same as visible, though. The UI draws the bar as a bare
width percentage with no number beside it, so simply holding the last
pre-1000 value across the drain looks exactly like a finished bar — the
symptom #90 is about. So the range is split instead: the unpack phase scales
to UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX (950 on wasm, upstream's 999 elsewhere) and the drain
animates the tail above it off the queue depth `flush_pending` now reports.
The bar only ever moves forward — the queue can grow while it drains, so the
raw ratio can fall.

Scaled, not clamped: the reader's old `min(1000 * read / size, 999)` capped a
value that overshoots the ceiling, which with a ceiling of 950 would park the
bar for the whole last twentieth of the read — the longest stretch of a big
restore. `min(MAX * read / size, MAX)` keeps it moving to the end. On native
that shifts reported progress by at most 1/1000th of the range; the 999
ceiling, the event sequence and the 0/1/1000 events upstream's tests assert
are unchanged.

`drain_progress` is a named function rather than an inline closure body so it
can carry `#[expect(clippy::arithmetic_side_effects)]`, the way three other
sites in this file already do — the lint is on for the crate and this patch
should stay upstreamable.
---
 src/imex.rs | 69 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 67 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/imex.rs b/src/imex.rs
index 4f2927f..239c2c3 100644
--- a/src/imex.rs
+++ b/src/imex.rs
@@ -247,6 +247,32 @@ pub(crate) async fn import_backup_stream<R: tokio::io::AsyncRead + Unpin>(
         .0
 }
 
+/// Highest progress the unpack phase of an import may report, leaving the rest
+/// of the 0..=1000 range for whatever still has to happen before the caller's
+/// final 1000. On wasm that tail is the OPFS drain (see
+/// `import_backup_stream_inner`), which is real waiting the user should see
+/// move; everywhere else nothing follows the unpack, so it keeps upstream's
+/// 999.
+#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]
+const UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX: u16 = 950;
+#[cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))]
+const UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX: u16 = 999;
+
+/// Maps the OPFS queue depth `flush_pending` reports onto the tail reserved
+/// above [`UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX`]. Tops out at 999 — the caller's final 1000 is
+/// what completes the bar — and an empty queue at entry means the whole tail is
+/// already earned. `remaining` can exceed `initial` (the queue grows while it
+/// drains), which floors the result rather than going backwards.
+#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]
+#[expect(clippy::arithmetic_side_effects)]
+fn drain_progress(remaining: usize, initial: usize) -> u16 {
+    let span = 999 - UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX;
+    if remaining == 0 || initial == 0 {
+        return UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX + span;
+    }
+    UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX + (span as usize * initial.saturating_sub(remaining) / initial) as u16
+}
+
 /// Reader that emits progress events as bytes are read from it.
 #[pin_project]
 struct ProgressReader<R> {
@@ -298,7 +324,11 @@ fn poll_read(
                 .read
                 .saturating_add(usize_to_u64(buf.filled().len() - before));
 
-            let progress = std::cmp::min(1000 * *this.read / *this.file_size, 999) as u16;
+            // scaled to the reserved ceiling rather than clamped at it: a
+            // clamp would park the bar there for the whole last 1/20th of the
+            // read, which is the longest stretch of a big restore
+            let max = u64::from(UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX);
+            let progress = std::cmp::min(max * *this.read / *this.file_size, max) as u16;
             if progress > *this.last_progress {
                 this.context.emit_event(EventType::ImexProgress(progress));
                 *this.last_progress = progress;
@@ -376,7 +406,7 @@ async fn import_backup_stream_inner<R: tokio::io::AsyncRead + Unpin>(
         .log_err(context)
         .ok();
     if res.is_ok() {
-        context.emit_event(EventType::ImexProgress(999));
+        context.emit_event(EventType::ImexProgress(UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX));
         res = context.sql.run_migrations(context).await;
         context.emit_event(EventType::AccountsItemChanged);
     }
@@ -402,6 +432,41 @@ async fn import_backup_stream_inner<R: tokio::io::AsyncRead + Unpin>(
             .log_err(context)
             .ok();
     }
+    // On wasm the unpacked blobs only live in the memfs until the async OPFS
+    // flusher writes them through. A reload before that queue drains rebuilds
+    // the memfs from OPFS *without* them, so a just-restored backup loses its
+    // pictures until they are fetched from the server again (#77). Draining
+    // here — before the caller emits ImexProgress(1000) — is what makes "100%"
+    // mean "durable", for the file import and the second-device transfer
+    // receive alike, both of which land in this function.
+    //
+    // The drain gets the reserved UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX..=999 tail of the range
+    // rather than sitting on one value: the UI draws the bar as a bare width
+    // percentage with no number next to it, so a bar parked at 99.9% is
+    // indistinguishable from a finished one, and the whole point is that this
+    // wait is visible rather than dead time behind a full bar.
+    //
+    // Cancelling (stopOngoingProcess) during the drain drops this future, and
+    // the drop skips the res.is_err() rollback below — as it does for any
+    // cancel past the import, this is just the widest window for it. The
+    // account stays imported and the flusher keeps draining in the background;
+    // only the reported outcome says "canceled".
+    #[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]
+    if res.is_ok() {
+        let mut last = UNPACK_PROGRESS_MAX;
+        // Baseline 0 = the count of lost write-throughs is deliberately
+        // ignored here: the web app diffs `fs_failed()` across the whole call
+        // and warns the user, and a partly-lost restore must not roll the
+        // imported account back, so it must not reach `res` either.
+        let _ = tokio::fs::flush_pending(0, |remaining, initial| {
+            let progress = drain_progress(remaining, initial);
+            if progress > last {
+                last = progress;
+                context.emit_event(EventType::ImexProgress(progress));
+            }
+        })
+        .await;
+    }
     (res,)
 }
 
